<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1128000" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/14062118/Tironian_Notes_Codex_Casselanus.png" alt width="600" height="802" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/14062118/Tironian_Notes_Codex_Casselanus.png 600w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/14062118/Tironian_Notes_Codex_Casselanus-269x360.png 269w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/14062118/Tironian_Notes_Codex_Casselanus-180x240.png 180w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px"></p>
<p>You can still type a symbol originally invented for Cicero nearly 21 centuries ago — at least if you configure your keyboard properly.&nbsp; The character ⁊ appears in Unicode as U+204A, but is better known as the “Tironian et”: the “et” is Latin for “and,” and the “Tironian” comes from its creator, a man named Tiro, or in full, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicero">Marcus Tullius Tiro</a>. Tiro wasn’t a relative of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicero">Marcus Tullius Cicero</a>, as you might expect, but rather his onetime slave: after being emancipated, he not only took on two of his former master’s names, but also remained his secretary and confidant. It was in that capacity that, at Cicero’s request, he created the system since known as <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tironian_notes">Tironian notes</a>.</p>
<p>Though now credited as “the father of stenography,” Tiro didn’t invent shorthand itself. Like so many developments in ancient Roman civilization, Hellenic inspiration was involved: no sooner did Cicero learn of the existence of Greek shorthand systems, so the story goes, than he asked Tiro to come up with one for Latin. It’s possible that he did so as early as 75 BC, when Cicero was assigned to Sicily and needed to encode his communications, especially those having to do with the corruption of local officials. Back in Rome, it became useful indeed for rapidly transcribing onto wax-covered tablets what the great orator had to say, in situations ranging from everyday dealings in the Forum to — as recorded by Plutarch — denouncing <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catilinarian_conspiracy">Catiline’s conspiracy to overthrow the Roman Republic</a>.</p>
